  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 330 AM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nThe upper level ridge will start to push back to the west some \nSunday evening/night as an upper level disturbance moves out of\nthe plains Sunday night into Monday. Thunderstorms will be \npossible across the central Plains ahead of the disturbance and\ncould develop into a complex of storms and move southeast into \nportions of the area Sunday evening/night into Monday morning. \nThere will be the potential for some strong to severe \nthunderstorms with this activity Sunday evening into Monday \nmorning. Damaging wind gusts will be the main risk, especially \nwith any line segments that can develop. This will be a complex \nof storms and the exact track will be dependent on just how far \nthe ridge pushed back to west Sunday night and also where the \nstorms develop before tracking to the east/southeast. The storms\ncould be banded in nature. Precipitable water values will be \naround 2\" so heavy rainfall rates will be possible with the \nstorms therefore the potential for locally heavy rainfall will \noccur, especially where any storms can training. There is \ncurrently a Marginal Risk for Severe storms and also a Marginal \nRisk for Excessive Rainfall across much of the area Sunday night\ninto Monday morning. \n\nSlightly cool temperatures are posisble across much of the area \non Monday, with highs in the middle 80s (central MO) to the \nmiddle 90s (extreme southeastern KS). Will have to watch just \nhow far back to the west the ridge pushes on Monday. If the \nridge pushes further to the west the hotter temperatures would\nalso push further back to the west, but if ridge remains further\nto the east highs would then be hotter to the east especially \nacross extreme southeastern Kansas and far southwestern Missouri\non Monday. The Ridge is then expected to build back to east on \nTuesday and over the entire area by Wednesday. Highs could \nrange from the upper 80s across central Missouri to near 100 \nacross extreme southeastern Kansas and far southwestern Missouri\non Tuesday. If the ridge does not push as far west Sunday \nnight/Monday the hotter conditions could move further east on \nTuesday as some of the ensemble model members bring the ridge \nfurther east on Tuesday. Highs in the middle 90s to around 100 \nare expected across the entire area on Wednesday.\n\nThe 00Z ensemble model members are then pushing the ridge back\nto the west late next week as an upper level shortwave moves \nsoutheast trough the region. This would push the hotter air \nmass back to the west and also bring some rain chances to the \narea late next week as the trough moves through the region. \nThere are still differences between the models on just how far \nwest the ridge pushes back especially from run to run.",
